option.CLOUD_BASE_DARK.comment = How much the underside of a cloud is darkened on top of everything else. The point is that this used to happen TWICE: multiple scattering already works with the optical depth straight upwards, so it knows exactly how much cloud stands above a point - that is the real occlusion. On top of that came a second darkening driven purely by height within the layer, which knows nothing about occlusion. It darkened the lower half of EVERY cloud, including a flank standing in full sunlight - seen from the side the cloud looked flat and shadowed below where nothing was shadowed. At 100 only the real occlusion decides. Higher values make clouds look more three-dimensional from the side and weaken the dark notch at the horizon, which is nothing but the underside of the deck.
option.CLOUD_THICK_BASE = Clouds: thickness
option.CLOUD_THICK_BASE.comment = Height of the cloud layer in blocks, relative to size 100 percent. At smaller sizes it is divided by the same factor, so the proportion stays constant. 87 was the old value and gave a 2.6 to 1 ratio against 229 blocks of width - flat. 130 gives 1.8 to 1, noticeably more volume at the same width.
value.CLOUD_THICK_BASE.70 = Very flat
value.CLOUD_THICK_BASE.87 = Flat (old value)
value.CLOUD_THICK_BASE.130 =默认
value.CLOUD_THICK_BASE.180 = Tall
value.CLOUD_THICK_BASE.250 = Towers
option.CLOUD_SIZE = Clouds: size
option.CLOUD_SIZE.comment = How large a single cloud is, as a percentage of the previous value. 100 is about 229 blocks across, 50 is 114. The slider scales the base shape, the Worley erosion and the strength of the swirl TOGETHER - the ratio of cloud width to detail size therefore stays constant and smaller clouds do not lose their puffs. That is exactly what went wrong on 25.07.2026, when only the base shape was scaled and the clouds turned smooth. Costs nothing: it is only a different sampling frequency, not an extra sample.
value.CLOUD_SIZE.100 = 100 % (229 bl, as before)
value.CLOUD_SIZE.75 = 75 % (172 bl)
value.CLOUD_SIZE.50 = 50 % (114 bl, default)
value.CLOUD_SIZE.35 = 35 % (80 bl)
value.CLOUD_SIZE.25 = 25 % (57 bl, very small)
option.CLOUD_COV_CELL = Clouds: region size
option.CLOUD_COV_CELL.comment = How large the regions of equal cloud cover are - and the actual reason all clouds could end up the same size. Coverage decides at which value the base shape is thresholded: just above the threshold only small isolated patches remain, well above it they grow into connected masses. This used to sit at about 4963 blocks while the visible cloud layer only spans some 3000, so a single view showed exactly one coverage value. Smaller means several regions at once: dense masses next to scattered small shapes. Below 596 blocks (the wavelength of the base shape) it stops making sense.
value.CLOUD_COV_CELL.4963 = 4963 (as before)
value.CLOUD_COV_CELL.3200 = 3200
value.CLOUD_COV_CELL.2200 = 2200
value.CLOUD_COV_CELL.1600 = 1600 (default)
value.CLOUD_COV_CELL.1100 = 1100 (very mixed)
option.CLOUD_COV_SPREAD = Clouds: density contrast
option.CLOUD_COV_SPREAD.comment = How much cloud cover differs between regions, as a percentage of the range stored in the shape profile. Larger spreads the size distribution: thin regions get thinner and break up into small shapes, dense ones grow into larger masses. It is computed around the middle of the range, so average cloudiness stays the same. 100 matches the previous behaviour.
value.CLOUD_COV_SPREAD.100 = Even (as before)
value.CLOUD_COV_SPREAD.150 = Slightly mixed
value.CLOUD_COV_SPREAD.210 =默认
value.CLOUD_COV_SPREAD.280 = Strongly mixed
value.CLOUD_COV_SPREAD.380 =极端
option.CLOUD_SUN_DIM = Clouds: dimming at low sun
option.CLOUD_SUN_DIM.comment = How much of the physically correct dimming is applied. Direct irradiance falls to two percent of its noon value towards the horizon, and to twelve percent at cloud altitude. That is correct, but it only looks right if the eye can adapt - EmberVoid has no exposure adaptation, so applied in full it would give a dark sunset rather than a dramatic one. The COLOUR always comes fully from the physics regardless; this slider only affects brightness. Higher = more contrast between sunlit tops and the blue rest.
value.CLOUD_SUN_DIM.0 = Off (constant brightness)
value.CLOUD_SUN_DIM.30 =微妙
value.CLOUD_SUN_DIM.60 =默认
value.CLOUD_SUN_DIM.80 =强
value.CLOUD_SUN_DIM.100 = Fully physical
option.CLOUD_DETAIL_REACH = Clouds: detail reach
value.CLOUD_DETAIL_REACH.1 = 1: Short (legacy)
value.CLOUD_DETAIL_REACH.2 = 2: Medium
value.CLOUD_DETAIL_REACH.3 = 3: Far
value.CLOUD_DETAIL_REACH.4 = 4: Very far
option.CLOUD_DETAIL_REACH.comment = How far the fine erosion detail carries that frays the cloud blobs apart. It was always there, but only fully visible overhead and out to about 20 degrees elevation - anything flatter was smoothly nibbled, and since the layer curvature went to level 5 it is gone entirely at the cloud horizon. The fade has a real reason: one texel of the finest octave is 0.8 blocks across and therefore undersampled beyond about 950 blocks, where it starts to shimmer. Carrying it further is only safe because the grain (temporal jitter) actually works as of today, so the temporal average now gains genuine extra samples. IF IT SHIMMERS at the far cloud edge: step back one level, or raise the temporal averaging - both act on the same cause.
option.CLOUD_HL_ROLLOFF = Clouds: highlight rolloff
value.CLOUD_HL_ROLLOFF.0 = Off (can burn out)
value.CLOUD_HL_ROLLOFF.150 = 150: Strongly capped
value.CLOUD_HL_ROLLOFF.170 = 170: Noticeable
value.CLOUD_HL_ROLLOFF.190 = 190: Normal
value.CLOUD_HL_ROLLOFF.220 = 220: Weak
value.CLOUD_HL_ROLLOFF.260 = 260: Very weak
option.CLOUD_HL_ROLLOFF.comment = Stops sunlit clouds from burning out to pure white. The pack's tonemapper has its white point at 2.2 - anything above that is indistinguishable white, no matter how well the structure was computed. Until now there was no limit inside the cloud path itself: every time brightness rose elsewhere (sun colour, ambient, opacity, bloom) the clouds pushed past that edge again and the burn-out returned. This curve softly compresses the peaks towards the set value without ever quite reaching it, so nothing is clipped, and colour is preserved because only luminance is scaled. Lower values mean more drawing in bright clouds but less radiance.
option.CLOUD_DENSE = Clouds: opacity
value.CLOUD_DENSE.1 = 1: Hazy (legacy)
value.CLOUD_DENSE.2 = 2: Normal
value.CLOUD_DENSE.3 = 3: Solid
value.CLOUD_DENSE.4 = 4: Very solid
value.CLOUD_DENSE.5 = 5: Opaque
option.CLOUD_DENSE.comment = How opaque the clouds are. At "Hazy" a thin cloud only covers 79 percent looking straight down through the layer - which is why terrain shows through and the clouds read as mist. "Normal" gives 93 percent, "Solid" 98. TRADE-OFF: the more opaque, the less the view ray sees into the cloud - from "Solid" upwards a single march step already covers almost fully and the surface loses its visible structure. Dense cloud cores were never the problem; the dial mainly affects thin clouds and edges.
option.CLOUD_SHARPEN = Clouds: blur
value.CLOUD_SHARPEN.0 = 0: off
value.CLOUD_SHARPEN.12 = 12: Very weak
value.CLOUD_SHARPEN.22 = 22: Weak
value.CLOUD_SHARPEN.35 = 35: Medium
value.CLOUD_SHARPEN.60 = 60: Normal (default)
value.CLOUD_SHARPEN.100 = 100: Very strong (legacy)
option.CLOUD_SHARPEN.comment = How strongly the cloud buffer is smoothed spatially while being upscaled. The cloud march offsets its starting point per pixel, so neighbouring pixels sample different positions; this filter averages them and turns the fixed dither pattern into a smooth surface. Since the grain and the temporal averaging were removed (2026-09-25) this is the only smoothing the clouds get - hence the default of 60. Lower values are sharper but let the dither pattern show through.
option.CLOUD_TOP_SOFT = Clouds: soft tops
value.CLOUD_TOP_SOFT.1 = 1: Sharp (legacy)
value.CLOUD_TOP_SOFT.2 = 2: Slightly soft
value.CLOUD_TOP_SOFT.3 = 3: Soft
value.CLOUD_TOP_SOFT.4 = 4: Very soft
value.CLOUD_TOP_SOFT.5 = 5: Wispy
option.CLOUD_TOP_SOFT.comment = How far the cloud density thins out towards the top of the layer. It used to fall off only in the topmost tenth - with a 130 block layer that is 13 blocks, which from the side is effectively a sharp skin. That is exactly why the view switches from the sunlit top to the darker flank within a few screen rows, producing the hard streak. Real cloud tops are lumpy; higher levels imitate that and spread the transition. Soft zone: level 1 = 8 blocks, level 3 = 32, level 5 = 58. PRICE: softer tops mean less cloud mass - at level 3 about 9 percent less than before. Raise the cloud amount by one step to compensate.
option.CLOUD_CURVE = Clouds: layer curvature
value.CLOUD_CURVE.1 = 1: Flat (legacy)
value.CLOUD_CURVE.2 = 2: Slightly curved
value.CLOUD_CURVE.3 = 3: Medium
value.CLOUD_CURVE.4 = 4: Strong
value.CLOUD_CURVE.5 = 5: Very strong
option.CLOUD_CURVE.comment = How strongly the cloud layer curves away towards the horizon - and the dial against the horizontal streak at eye level. The streak appears because a horizontal view ray travels the LENGTH of the layer and stays inside it for kilometres, while its neighbour one degree above leaves it after a few hundred blocks. Along that long path the ray almost always finds cloud while the neighbour sees clear sky - which is why it stands out most when there are FEW clouds. Stronger curvature makes the deck tip away sooner: the long path shrinks, and above all the effect spreads over more angle. The sharp line becomes a wide, soft brightening. Measured band width: level 1 = 0.76 degrees, level 3 = 1.31, level 5 = 2.07. Level 5 requires the reach setting to be no higher than 2, otherwise the layer curves away before the distance fade is finished and a hard edge appears at the horizon.
option.CLOUD_REACH_PCT = Clouds: reach
value.CLOUD_REACH_PCT.60 =短
value.CLOUD_REACH_PCT.80 = Fairly short
value.CLOUD_REACH_PCT.100 = Matched to geometry
value.CLOUD_REACH_PCT.130 =远
value.CLOUD_REACH_PCT.160 =非常远
option.CLOUD_REACH_PCT.comment = Where the colour match completes, after which a cloud carries the sky's colour - as a factor on the distance at which the geometry changes character. That distance depends on where the camera is: standing INSIDE the layer, a downward ray only reaches the base above a certain angle; below it the ray misses the base, curves upward and exits through the top - the path length jumps several-fold there. This used to be a fixed number of blocks and could not hit that spot at all: too small left a GAP, too large left a LINE. Both were the same cause from opposite sides. At 100 the match completes exactly where the jump is, putting the jump entirely inside the haze. Lower fades earlier, higher lets the deck reach further.
