option.CLOUD_PHASE_FLOOR = Clouds: directional dependence
value.CLOUD_PHASE_FLOOR.70 = Strong (legacy)
value.CLOUD_PHASE_FLOOR.85 = Marked
value.CLOUD_PHASE_FLOOR.92 =中等
value.CLOUD_PHASE_FLOOR.96 =弱
value.CLOUD_PHASE_FLOOR.100 = Sunward glow only
option.CLOUD_PHASE_FLOOR.comment = How much cloud brightness changes with VIEW DIRECTION. Measured, the scattering term varied by a factor of 1.79 from this alone: 1.21 towards the sun but only 0.68 at right angles to it - which is why the same clouds looked bright or grey depending on where you faced, with no bright highlights at all in the dark direction. In an optically thick cloud, however, light is nearly evenly distributed after many scattering events; it cannot get that dark across the sun. Other shaders achieve this with several scattering orders, while our two-stream model keeps too much direction. This slider bounds the phase from below and leaves the sunward glow untouched - that one should stay, it is the silver lining. At Moderate the variation drops to 1.32.
option.CLOUD_MS_DEEP = Clouds: floor deep inside the cloud
value.CLOUD_MS_DEEP.0 = Off (can go black)
value.CLOUD_MS_DEEP.6 =弱
value.CLOUD_MS_DEEP.12 =中等
value.CLOUD_MS_DEEP.20 =强
value.CLOUD_MS_DEEP.30 =非常强
option.CLOUD_MS_DEEP.comment = A lower bound on the light deep inside a cloud. The scattering model uses two orders, and their sum decays towards ZERO with optical depth - at depth 100 only 5.6 percent remains, at 200 just 0.8. A point deep inside the deck therefore receives no light at all and turns black. Grazing rays near the horizon travel furthest through the deck, which is exactly where the dark part sits. Physically this is wrong: cloud droplets have an albedo of about 0.9999 in the visible, so light inside a cloud is hardly absorbed at all - it is redistributed, approaching a diffusion limit rather than zero. Shaders using several scattering orders keep that floor by themselves. This slider acts ONLY where the two orders already deliver almost nothing: at Moderate from optical depth 63 upwards, below which nothing changes. Bright or thin clouds are unaffected.
option.CLOUD_FINE_DETAIL = Fine third erosion octave
option.CLOUD_LIGHT_EROSION = Erosion in the light march too
option.CLOUD_LIGHT_3SAMPLE = Light march with 3 samples
option.SSR_DEBUG = SSR diagnostics
option.SSR_DEBUG.comment = Colours every reflection pixel by the code path it took - on water AND on wet blocks. Legend: GREEN real hit. YELLOW hit, but heavily damped at the screen edge. BLUE nothing hit, steps exhausted. ORANGE the ray left the screen. MAGENTA the reflection target lies behind the camera. CYAN a coplanar graze was rejected and nothing was found afterwards. WHITE Fresnel below the threshold, no march was done at all. GREY hit on water. RED close-range self hit. PINK should never appear. To pin down flicker, watch WHICH colour changes as you turn the camera. Blocks are only coloured while they are wet, so it needs rain.
