value.CLOUD_TAIL_EXP.3.5 =高
value.CLOUD_TAIL_EXP.5.0 =极高
option.CLOUD_TAIL_EXP.comment = At what height the cloud turns into the tail. Low makes the whole cloud stringy (that gave the thin slices), high leaves a compact puff with only a plume drawing off the top.
option.CLOUD_DIR_VAR = Clouds: direction variation
value.CLOUD_DIR_VAR.0 =关
value.CLOUD_DIR_VAR.25 =§aSlight
value.CLOUD_DIR_VAR.55 =中
value.CLOUD_DIR_VAR.90 =强
value.CLOUD_DIR_VAR.140 = Chaotic
option.CLOUD_DIR_VAR.comment = Without this all tails drift in parallel because they hang off a single wind direction - the sky then feels prescribed. Here a coarse noise field rotates the drift direction by location. The clouds' overall drift stays global, only the shape wanders. At 0 the texture fetch is skipped.
option.CLOUD_DIR_CELL = Clouds: variation size
value.CLOUD_DIR_CELL.3000 = 3000 bl
value.CLOUD_DIR_CELL.1400 = 1400 bl
value.CLOUD_DIR_CELL.700 = 700 bl
option.CLOUD_DIR_CELL.comment = Over what distance the drift direction changes. Large = neighbouring clouds still travel together, small = even direct neighbours can wander off in different directions.
option.SHADOW_FILTER = Shadow: filter pattern
option.SHADOW_FILTER.comment = Sampling pattern for the shadow edge. Fixed 3x3 is the crisp EmberVoid pixel look: nine points, always in the same places, which gives stepped edges and a striped pattern on sloped surfaces. Rotated disc samples points on a spiral and rotates the pattern per world position - soft, stepless edges with no preferred direction. The rotation is anchored to the world, so it does not shimmer while walking or as the sun moves. Smooth (default since 0.5.4): a fixed 4x4 grid with hardware comparison, no randomness and no rotation - a stepless penumbra without the fine grid of the rotated disc, which changes its rotation every 1/64 block. With Smooth the sample-point setting does not apply; it always uses 16.
value.SHADOW_FILTER.0 = Fixed 3x3 (pixel look)
value.SHADOW_FILTER.1 = Rotated disc (old)
value.SHADOW_FILTER.2 = Smooth (default)
option.SHADOW_NEAREST = Shadow: texture filter
option.SHADOW_NEAREST.comment = This setting was missing entirely, so Iris filtered the shadow map LINEARLY - averaging four depth values. An averaged depth value belongs to no existing surface. At block edges, where depth jumps, this produces in-between values and the light/shadow comparison flips for a narrow strip. For a depth map, nearest is the only correct choice; the soft edge comes from the blur, and that averages decisions instead of depths.
value.SHADOW_NEAREST.0 =线性
value.SHADOW_NEAREST.1 = Nearest
option.SUN_SKY_HARD = Shadow: hard sun threshold
option.SUN_SKY_HARD.comment = Replaces the soft skylight factor on direct sunlight with a threshold. Prevents sunlight from appearing in caves where the shadow map does not reach. Above roughly two light levels the factor is a constant 1.0, so the interpolation gradient across a face disappears.
option.SKY_CAP = Shadow: skylight cap
option.SKY_CAP.comment = UNCONFIRMED TOOL, off by default. Was meant to help against the bright light gaps at low sun; it demonstrably does not - diagnostic mode 13 shows that the affected spots see open sky. At grazing light the shadow map has holes by design - a block top then covers barely any texels, and the vertical sides between them do not exist at all. This slider additionally brings in the vanilla skylight: a block that barely sees sky cannot be fully sunlit. Only acts at low sun. Price: if the low sun shines horizontally under an overhang, that spot becomes too dark. Diagnostic mode 13 shows red where the cap takes effect.
value.SKY_CAP.0 =关
value.SKY_CAP.40 =弱
value.SKY_CAP.70 =中
value.SKY_CAP.100 =完整
option.SHADOW_DISTORT = Shadow: near-field resolution
option.SHADOW_DISTORT.comment = Redistributes the shadow map resolution: plenty up close, little in the distance. Without it a texel is 0.125 blocks everywhere, even right in front of you - hence the blocky edges and the strong flicker up close. At 88 a texel at the player is eight times finer than at the map edge, i.e. 0.015 blocks. Photon and Mellow have always done this; EmberVoid did not.
value.SHADOW_DISTORT.0 = Off (uniform)
value.SHADOW_DISTORT.50 = 2x near
value.SHADOW_DISTORT.65 = 3x near
value.SHADOW_DISTORT.80 = 5x near
value.SHADOW_DISTORT.88 = 8x near (default)
option.SHADOW_SNAP = Shadow: grid lock
option.SHADOW_SNAP.comment = ONLY TAKES EFFECT WITH near-field resolution OFF. Locks the texel grid to the world. Combined with distortion it does harm and is therefore disabled: the offset gets magnified eightfold up close and keeps sliding the map content back and forth by several near-field texels - that was the trembling of the shadow edges. Photon and Mellow do not snap at all, for the same reason. Without distortion it still holds: On = no crawling while walking but the map jumps with the sun; Off = the other way round.
value.SHADOW_SNAP.0 = Off (steady)
value.SHADOW_SNAP.1 = On (world-fixed)
option.SHADOW_SOFTNESS = Shadow: softness
option.SHADOW_SOFTNESS.comment = Radius of the shadow edge blur in texels. One texel is 0.125 blocks at the map edge, and finer near the player if near-field resolution is on. NOTE: the value is tied to the map resolution. The default of 1.6 gives 0.20 blocks; change the map and you have to follow here. Wider turns the bright seam at low sun into a soft gradient but costs sharpness. Must match the sample count.
value.SHADOW_SOFTNESS.1.0 = Very hard
value.SHADOW_SOFTNESS.1.6 = Default (crisp)
value.SHADOW_SOFTNESS.2.2 = Slightly softer
value.SHADOW_SOFTNESS.3.0 =柔和
value.SHADOW_SOFTNESS.4.5 =更柔和
value.SHADOW_SOFTNESS.6.0 =非常柔和
value.SHADOW_SOFTNESS.8.0 = Diffuse
value.SHADOW_SOFTNESS.11.0 = Very diffuse
value.SHADOW_SOFTNESS.14.0 = Extremely diffuse
option.SHADOW_SOFT_UNIFORM = Shadow: even out softness
option.SHADOW_SOFT_UNIFORM.comment = Compensates the blur against the near-field resolution. Without it the radius is measured in real texels, and since a texel is eight times finer up close the penumbra shrinks to an eighth there - the edge becomes a one-texel step that trembles whenever you move, while distant shadows end up softer than near ones. The value is the cap on the widening: 3 means at most a threefold radius up close. Higher is calmer but needs more samples, otherwise it grains.
value.SHADOW_SOFT_UNIFORM.0 = Off (sharpest edge)
value.SHADOW_SOFT_UNIFORM.2 = Up to 2x
value.SHADOW_SOFT_UNIFORM.3 = Up to 3x (default)
value.SHADOW_SOFT_UNIFORM.4 = Up to 4x
value.SHADOW_SOFT_UNIFORM.6 = Up to 6x
value.SHADOW_SOFT_UNIFORM.8 = Full (uniform)
option.SHADOW_TAPS = Shadow: sample points
option.SHADOW_TAPS.comment = How many points the blur samples. Too few at a large radius give noise instead of smoothing - rule of thumb about four points per texel of radius. Strongest FPS control in the shadow path; with coloured shadows it is three texture fetches per point.
value.SHADOW_TAPS.8 = 8 (default)
value.SHADOW_TAPS.12 = 12 (old)
value.SHADOW_TAPS.16 = 16
value.SHADOW_TAPS.24 = 24
value.SHADOW_TAPS.32 = 32 (expensive)
option.SHADOW_RPDB = Shadow: receiver plane bias
option.SHADOW_RPDB.comment = Against the bright seams at low sun. Off = the depth threshold is estimated from the angle and clamped; the clamp leaves a fixed 0.125-block floor that no slider can reach, and that is what leaks light at the terminator. On = the depth gradient is measured, the centre tap gets zero bias.
value.SHADOW_RPDB.0 = Off (estimated)
value.SHADOW_RPDB.1 = On (measured)
option.SHADOW_CENTRE_PULL = Shadow: pull toward block centre
option.SHADOW_CENTRE_PULL.comment = Against light leaks on the sides of blocks, especially deep underground. At low sun the shadow map is missing the occluder - it would be hundreds of blocks away and therefore outside its range. This slider pulls the lookup point into the block, where an occluder is guaranteed. Note: the offset jumps at every block boundary, which produces square steps in the shadow edge - hence off by default.
value.SHADOW_CENTRE_PULL.0 =关
value.SHADOW_CENTRE_PULL.10 =弱
value.SHADOW_CENTRE_PULL.20 =中
value.SHADOW_CENTRE_PULL.35 =强
value.SHADOW_CENTRE_PULL.50 =非常强
option.CAVE_LIGHTING = Caves: decouple from time of day
option.CAVE_LIGHTING.comment = Whether caves and windowless interiors stay equally dark regardless of the time of day. Without this option the same deep cave was 35 times brighter at noon than on a new moon night, even though no daylight reaches it - two terms of the daytime lighting applied even with no sky access at all. The transition runs off sky access itself, not off altitude: the surface is therefore unaffected, while a windowless house is - and that is correct.
option.CAVE_AMBIENT = Caves: ambient level
value.CAVE_AMBIENT.0 = Pitch black
value.CAVE_AMBIENT.40 =极暗
value.CAVE_AMBIENT.70 =暗
value.CAVE_AMBIENT.100 =普通
value.CAVE_AMBIENT.150 =明亮
value.CAVE_AMBIENT.220 =极亮
option.CAVE_AMBIENT.comment = How bright it is deep underground with no light source at all. "Normal" matches what a cave used to have on a full moon night - now at any time of day. "Pitch black" makes torches the only light source. Block light is unaffected, and in caves it is no longer halved the way it is on the surface.
option.SUN_LAMBERT = Sun: angular falloff
option.SUN_LAMBERT.comment = How much a surface darkens as sunlight hits it at a steeper angle. Off = old behaviour, full brightness from about 70 degrees of slant onwards; grazing surfaces end up to 3x too bright, which is what creates the bright seams at low sun. On = cosine falloff as in nature. Mornings and evenings get darker overall.
value.SUN_LAMBERT.0 = Off (old)
value.SUN_LAMBERT.1 = On (Lambert)
option.SHADOW_DEPTH_BIAS = Shadow: depth threshold
value.SHADOW_DEPTH_BIAS.0.005 = 0.005 bl
value.SHADOW_DEPTH_BIAS.0.01 = 0.01
value.SHADOW_DEPTH_BIAS.0.015 = 0.015
value.SHADOW_DEPTH_BIAS.0.02 = 0.02
value.SHADOW_DEPTH_BIAS.0.03 = 0.03
value.SHADOW_DEPTH_BIAS.0.05 =0.05
value.SHADOW_DEPTH_BIAS.0.08 = 0.08 bl
option.SHADOW_DEPTH_BIAS.comment = In BLOCKS. From what distance an obstacle counts as shadow casting. The reference is the texel size - at default settings a texel is 0.125 blocks. Too small gives grainy speckle, too large detaches shadows at contact points. This value used to be in a unit that meant anywhere between 0.10 and 0.41 blocks depending on range - which is why it was never reproducibly adjustable.
option.SHADOW_BIAS = Shadow: normal offset
value.SHADOW_BIAS.0 =0%
value.SHADOW_BIAS.15 =15%
value.SHADOW_BIAS.30 =30%
value.SHADOW_BIAS.45 =45%
value.SHADOW_BIAS.60 =60%
value.SHADOW_BIAS.80 = 80%
value.SHADOW_BIAS.100 = 100%
value.SHADOW_BIAS.130 = 130%
value.SHADOW_BIAS.170 = 170%
option.SHADOW_BIAS.comment = How far a surface is pushed out of its own shadow map texel. Too little gives grainy speckle in shadowed areas, too much shifts shadow edges sideways. Since 10.08.2026 the offset runs only across the light and can therefore no longer shorten the shadow.
option.CLOUD_STAGE_DEBUG = Debug: cloud density by stage
value.CLOUD_STAGE_DEBUG.0 = 0: off
value.CLOUD_STAGE_DEBUG.1 = 1: coverage noise
value.CLOUD_STAGE_DEBUG.2 = 2: base shape
value.CLOUD_STAGE_DEBUG.3 = 3: after coverage threshold
value.CLOUD_STAGE_DEBUG.4 = 4: erosion
option.CLOUD_STAGE_DEBUG.comment = Paints ONE stage of the cloud density field across the terrain - seen from above, a map of the field. Whichever stage the angular tiles first appear in is the cause. Use it from above, looking at open ground, and after a few minutes of play: the wind carries the sampling position, so right after loading the fault is not visible. The stages come from the same function that runs in earnest - no second copy.
option.CSHADOW_DEBUG = Diagnostics: cloud shadows / god rays
value.CSHADOW_DEBUG.0 = 0: off
value.CSHADOW_DEBUG.1 = 1: cloud shadow
value.CSHADOW_DEBUG.2 = 2: godray shadow
value.CSHADOW_DEBUG.3 = 3: godray only
option.CSHADOW_DEBUG.comment = Shows the two sun-dependent terms that are only applied to the image in composite.fsh. AA_DEBUG in gbuffers_terrain cannot show them.
option.GODRAY_CONTRAST = Light: shaft contrast
value.GODRAY_CONTRAST.0 =关
value.GODRAY_CONTRAST.120 =弱
value.GODRAY_CONTRAST.250 =普通
value.GODRAY_CONTRAST.400 =强
value.GODRAY_CONTRAST.650 =非常强
value.GODRAY_CONTRAST.900 =极端
option.GODRAY_CONTRAST.comment = How strongly a light shaft stands out from its surroundings. Separate from the overall strength: that controls the flat glow under a clear sky, this slider only the visible shafts. Under a clear sky with no clouds it has no effect - there is nothing there for a shaft to stand out from.
option.EMBER_DEBUG = Diagnostics: ember sparks
option.EMBER_DEBUG.comment = The sparks were invisible twice in a row, each time for a different reason - first the spark point sat in a 3D cell that a view ray cannot sample at all, then density, size and brightness were all chosen too cautiously at once. Mode 1 shows only the spark field in white, without the time window and without the horizon band, so it is visible at any time of day: if there is nothing there, the field itself is broken rather than the tuning. Mode 2 shows the time window as a grey value across the whole sky - black means it is not spark time right now.
value.EMBER_DEBUG.0 =关
value.EMBER_DEBUG.1 = Spark field only
value.EMBER_DEBUG.2 = Time window
option.EMBER_TRAIL = Twilight: spark trail
option.EMBER_TRAIL.comment = How far the trail glows behind a rising spark, as a multiple of the head radius. The head is about 2.4 px across, so 5 gives roughly a 12 px trail. It fades out downwards by itself. Longer trails make the sparks feel faster even though they rise at the same speed.
value.EMBER_TRAIL.2 = Very short
value.EMBER_TRAIL.3 =短
value.EMBER_TRAIL.5 =默认
value.EMBER_TRAIL.8 =长
value.EMBER_TRAIL.12 =非常长
option.EMBER_STRENGTH = Twilight: ember sparks
option.EMBER_STRENGTH.comment = Rising embers in the twilight air, close above the horizon and denser towards the sun. Deliberately kept fine: about one pixel across, brightness distributed quadratically (most sparks very faint, a few bright) and a band that thins out towards the top - it should read as texture, not as a particle cloud. The colour is the same as the ember rim on the clouds and the sun disc, so it is the same material in a different form. Only appears at twilight, and not in rain.
value.EMBER_STRENGTH.0 =关
value.EMBER_STRENGTH.25 = Barely
value.EMBER_STRENGTH.50 =默认
value.EMBER_STRENGTH.75 = Noticeable
value.EMBER_STRENGTH.100 =强
option.SKY_TWILIGHT = Twilight: residual sky brightness
option.SKY_TWILIGHT.comment = How bright the upper sky stays after sunset. Until now the zenith followed the sun elevation directly and reached its night colour at the exact moment the sun crossed the horizon - while the horizon was still glowing. That gave a hard black lid over the sunset. In reality the upper sky stays lit for a long time because the high atmosphere is still in sunlight: air at 30 km altitude still sees the sun when it is 5.6 degrees below the horizon for the ground. 0 restores the old behaviour.
value.SKY_TWILIGHT.0 = Off (as before)
value.SKY_TWILIGHT.15 =微妙
value.SKY_TWILIGHT.25 =默认
value.SKY_TWILIGHT.40 =长
value.SKY_TWILIGHT.60 =非常长
option.SUNSET_WIDTH = Sunset glow: width
value.SUNSET_WIDTH.1 = Very wide (legacy)
value.SUNSET_WIDTH.2 =宽阔
value.SUNSET_WIDTH.3 = Narrow
value.SUNSET_WIDTH.5 = Very narrow
option.SUNSET_WIDTH.comment = How much of the sky the sunset glow covers. The value is an exponent on proximity to the sun and narrows the effect without weakening the sun side itself - straight towards the sun the weight always stays full. At "Very wide" the sky at right angles to the sun still carries 60 percent of the glow, at "Wide" 40, at "Narrow" 30. The 20 percent floor opposite the sun remains at every setting: that is where the Earth's shadow arc stands in reality, with the counter-twilight glow above it.
option.SUNSET_SAT = Sunset: intensity
option.SUNSET_SAT.comment = How saturated the evening sky becomes. At 100 the shader uses pure single scattering, which runs towards almost pure red at the horizon and looks aggressive spread across half the sky. Real skylight is scattered many times, and each further scattering flattens the spectrum; lower values model that. The HUE does not change, only the saturation - the evening stays in the same colour family. Affects horizon fog, sky and light shafts. The sun disc and the clouds are untouched: they are lit by the direct beam and are allowed to go deep red.
value.SUNSET_SAT.30 =非常柔和
value.SUNSET_SAT.45 =柔和
value.SUNSET_SAT.60 =默认
value.SUNSET_SAT.75 =强
value.SUNSET_SAT.100 = Raw (single scattering)
option.GODRAY_WIDE = Light: shaft width
option.GODRAY_WIDE.comment = How much surroundings a light shaft has to stand out against, in screen pixels. The contrast is computed as a difference of two scales and therefore only responds to structures between them - if the reference is narrower than the shaft, the shaft shows only its rim instead of its body. Wider therefore means the shaft glows as a solid beam rather than an outline. Turned all the way up the reference approaches the screen average and the shafts may pulse slightly as you turn.
value.GODRAY_WIDE.3 = 16 px (rims only)
value.GODRAY_WIDE.4 = 32 px
value.GODRAY_WIDE.5 = 64 px (default)
value.GODRAY_WIDE.6 = 128 px (solid)
option.STATIC_SHADOW_WIDTH = Shadow: far shadow transition
value.STATIC_SHADOW_WIDTH.0.14 = 0.14
value.STATIC_SHADOW_WIDTH.0.25 =0.25
value.STATIC_SHADOW_WIDTH.0.40 = 0.40
value.STATIC_SHADOW_WIDTH.0.60 = 0.60
option.STATIC_SHADOW_WIDTH.comment = Beyond the shadow map the shadow is derived from the vanilla skylight, and that is quantised into 16 levels. A narrow transition makes neighbouring blocks differ a lot in brightness - those are the bright streaks at low sun. Wider spreads the transition over more light levels. Too wide makes distant overhangs and cave entrances too bright.
option.PLANT_SHADOW_SIDE = Shadow: plant side offset
value.PLANT_SHADOW_SIDE.0.0 =0.00
value.PLANT_SHADOW_SIDE.0.15 = 0.15 bl
value.PLANT_SHADOW_SIDE.0.30 = 0.30 bl
value.PLANT_SHADOW_SIDE.0.45 = 0.45 bl
option.PLANT_SHADOW_SIDE.comment = Plants query their shadow at the block centre so that swaying blades do not cause flicker. A small offset towards the sun helps so a neighbouring blade does not shadow them. Above 0.5 the lookup point leaves its own block and reads neighbouring geometry - that produced the bright patches at low sun. At 0 it stays exactly above the block.

option.GLOSS_SSR_MODE = Gloss: reflection mode on blocks
option.GLOSS_SSR_MODE.comment = How reflections on wet and smooth blocks are produced. An upward-facing wet block mostly reflects sky, and the shader can compute that straight from the reflection direction - no ray march, no hit decision, and therefore no flicker by construction. Clouds are included. The full screen-space march additionally reflects terrain, but has to decide per pixel whether the ray hits something or leaves the screen border, and that seam can flicker as you turn the camera. Water surfaces are not affected by this setting; they always march.
value.GLOSS_SSR_MODE.0 =关
value.GLOSS_SSR_MODE.1 = Sky only (stable)
value.GLOSS_SSR_MODE.2 = Full (screen-space, may flicker)

option.GLOSS_SSR_SMOOTH = Gloss: smooth reflection
option.GLOSS_SSR_SMOOTH.comment = How strongly the surface normal used for the reflection is smoothed towards the block's geometric face. It sounds minor but it is the difference between a calm and a jumping reflection: the normal is stored with only 8 bits, one single bit step is 0.9 degrees of reflection direction - and at grazing angles onto the cloud layer that shifts the sampled cloud point by several cloud widths. The same applies to the ripple of the rain film. High values calm this down at the cost of ripple detail in the reflection; for a thin film on stone a nearly flat mirror is the right look anyway. Affects blocks only, not water surfaces.
value.GLOSS_SSR_SMOOTH.45 = Barely (ripple visible)
value.GLOSS_SSR_SMOOTH.65 =§aSlight
value.GLOSS_SSR_SMOOTH.85 = Default (calm)
value.GLOSS_SSR_SMOOTH.95 = Mirror flat

option.WIND_GUST = Waves: gusts
value.WIND_GUST.0 =关
value.WIND_GUST.25 =光照
value.WIND_GUST.45 =普通
value.WIND_GUST.70 =强
value.WIND_GUST.100 =非常强
option.WIND_GUST.comment = Makes wave height vary on a large scale, so the water has smooth and roughened patches. Without it the surface is exactly equally rough everywhere, because a sum of fixed waves has the same strength at every point. Sailors call the dark patches cat's paws.
option.WIND_FIELD = Waves: wind field
value.WIND_FIELD.0 =关
value.WIND_FIELD.25 =弱
value.WIND_FIELD.55 =普通
value.WIND_FIELD.90 =强
value.WIND_FIELD.140 =非常强
option.WIND_FIELD.comment = Warps the wave map on a large scale, fixed to the world, as if the wind curved across the landscape. Without it wave crests are mathematically straight lines and form a visibly repeating lattice in the distance. Every bay gets its own wave pattern. Higher values bend more.
option.FOAM_SCALE = Shore foam: grain size
value.FOAM_SCALE.70 = Very coarse
value.FOAM_SCALE.100 =粗略
value.FOAM_SCALE.150 =中
value.FOAM_SCALE.200 = Fine
value.FOAM_SCALE.300 = Very fine
option.FOAM_SCALE.comment = How fine the foam flecks are. The slider zooms the ENTIRE structure: both noise layers, the bubble field and the drift speed scale with it. Otherwise it would not be a zoom but a shift in proportions - at very fine the flecks would end up the same size as the bubble holes and the structure collapses. At "Fine" a fleck is about 0.16 blocks across. Very fine grain fades out earlier with distance, otherwise it aliases into shimmering lines.
option.FOAM_BURST = Shore foam: bursting
value.FOAM_BURST.0 =关
value.FOAM_BURST.40 =弱
value.FOAM_BURST.70 =中
value.FOAM_BURST.100 =强
option.FOAM_BURST.comment = Whether individual foam patches run their own life cycle: rise, hold briefly, thin out, vanish - and the neighbouring patch two seconds later. Without it the whole foam breathes in one rhythm. Implemented through the threshold rather than the size: a patch retreats from its edges as it goes and breaks into smaller pieces, instead of fading uniformly. The average amount of foam stays the same; that is compensated numerically.
option.FOAM_POOL = Shore foam: minimum water size
value.FOAM_POOL.0 =关
value.FOAM_POOL.20 = Small (2 blocks)
value.FOAM_POOL.30 = Medium (3 blocks)
value.FOAM_POOL.45 = Large (4.5 blocks)
option.FOAM_POOL.comment = How large a body of water has to be before shore foam appears. Surf looks wrong in a 2x2 puddle. Openness is measured on two rings, a fixed small one and one of the given radius: eight points per ring, one single question - is there water there? Two rings are needed because a large one falls entirely off screen up close, while a small one cannot tell a 4x4 puddle from a lake. Combined they yield 0.00 for puddles up to 4x4, 0.25 at the edge of a 6x6 puddle, 0.50 on a river five blocks wide and at a lake shore, 0.75 three blocks out. Affects only the shore band; crest foam on breaking waves is untouched. Costs eight depth lookups, but only near the shore.
option.FOAM_WIDTH = Shore foam: width
value.FOAM_WIDTH.0.35 = Very narrow
value.FOAM_WIDTH.0.55 = Narrow
value.FOAM_WIDTH.0.75 =普通
value.FOAM_WIDTH.1.10 =宽阔
value.FOAM_WIDTH.1.60 = Very wide
option.FOAM_WIDTH.comment = Up to what water depth surf appears, in blocks. Measured straight down, so the band follows the underwater terrain rather than the viewing angle.
option.FOAM_FLOW = Shore foam: current
value.FOAM_FLOW.0 = Off (rigid)
value.FOAM_FLOW.35 =弱
value.FOAM_FLOW.70 =中
value.FOAM_FLOW.110 =强
value.FOAM_FLOW.160 =非常强
option.FOAM_FLOW.comment = Whether the foam is dragged by a current. "Off" translates the grain pattern as a whole - every fleck keeps its shape forever and the field slides across the water like a printed sheet. Otherwise two time-offset layers run, each warped along the current and cross-faded: the foam is pulled toward the shore and back out, dissolves gradually and reforms elsewhere. Costs four noise lookups instead of two.
option.FOAM_SURGE = Shore foam: surge
value.FOAM_SURGE.0 =关
value.FOAM_SURGE.30 =平静
value.FOAM_SURGE.55 =普通
value.FOAM_SURGE.85 = Lively
value.FOAM_SURGE.120 =风暴
option.FOAM_SURGE.comment = Makes the foam wash in and out instead of standing still as a fixed rim, with the surge travelling along the shore. Without it the foam reads as paint on the shoreline rather than water running over it.
option.FOAM_DRAG = Shore foam: undertow
value.FOAM_DRAG.0 =关
value.FOAM_DRAG.30 = Little
value.FOAM_DRAG.60 =普通
value.FOAM_DRAG.100 = Much
value.FOAM_DRAG.150 = Very much
option.FOAM_DRAG.comment = How far the retreating wave drags foam out onto open water. It returns as the wave runs up again. Without it only the band's width breathes while the foam inside stands still.
option.PLANT_SHADOW_SNAP = Plants: shadow anchor
value.PLANT_SHADOW_SNAP.0 =平滑
value.PLANT_SHADOW_SNAP.1 = Per block (legacy)
option.PLANT_SHADOW_SNAP.comment = Where the shadow for grass and flowers is sampled. "Per block" rounds to the block centre, which makes a whole grass field bright or dark in block-sized steps that jump as you walk. "Smooth" follows the plant continuously and gives a gradient instead of an edge.
option.CANE_SWAY = Sugar cane: sway
value.CANE_SWAY.0 =关
value.CANE_SWAY.12 = Very little
value.CANE_SWAY.22 = Little
value.CANE_SWAY.35 =普通
value.CANE_SWAY.55 = Much
option.CANE_SWAY.comment = How strongly sugar cane sways in the wind. The base cannot be anchored completely: the model only has vertices on the block boundaries, and if the offset were zero there the whole stalk would stand still. Low values keep base movement below half a texture pixel.
option.FOAM_SHAPE = Shore foam: shape measure
value.FOAM_SHAPE.0 = Legacy (vertical depth)
value.FOAM_SHAPE.1 = Horizontal shore distance
option.FOAM_SHAPE.comment = What the surf zone is measured against. "Legacy" uses the water depth straight below the point. That value is identical across a whole block face and jumps by a full block at every block edge, so the foam inevitably traces the block silhouette and looks squashed. "Horizontal shore distance" samples the surroundings and measures how far the shoreline is. That is continuous and also the physically correct measure, but it costs extra depth lookups per water pixel near the shore (count under "Shore foam: sample count"). It does not make the band smooth - the ground stays quantised to whole blocks in height - but the remaining steps are much smaller and no longer sit on the block edges.
option.FOAM_TAPS = Shore foam: sample count
value.FOAM_TAPS.8 = 8 taps (cheap)
value.FOAM_TAPS.12 = 12 taps
value.FOAM_TAPS.20 = 20 taps
value.FOAM_TAPS.32 = 32 taps (smooth)
option.FOAM_TAPS.comment = How many depth lookups the shore shape does per water pixel near the shore. More taps means a smoother foam band. Measured on a model coast, the largest remaining step in foam coverage is 0.79 at 12 taps, 0.48 at 20 and 0.32 at 32 - the benefit drops off sharply while the cost grows linearly. Only active with shape measure "Horizontal shore distance".
option.FOAM_AMOUNT = Water: foam amount
option.FOAM_AMOUNT.comment = How much shore and crest foam forms. The foam is built grainy: two counter-drifting noise layers through a narrow threshold window, with the threshold moving with proximity to the shore. Right at the shore almost everything passes, further out only the peaks remain - so the edge breaks up into individual flecks instead of fading out as a sheet. That is exactly what separates foam from a bright film. Its brightness follows the light, so it stays correct at any time of day.
value.FOAM_AMOUNT.0 =关
value.FOAM_AMOUNT.40 =非常微弱
value.FOAM_AMOUNT.65 = Default (subtle)
value.FOAM_AMOUNT.100 =晴朗
value.FOAM_AMOUNT.150 = Lots
value.FOAM_AMOUNT.220 = Surf
option.FOAM_MATT = Water: foam matting
option.FOAM_MATT.comment = How strongly the foam dulls the surface. Foam is rough: it does not mirror and carries no specular highlight. This slider damps both at once - the surface reflection, the sun and moon highlight, and the screen-space reflection in the composite pass. At 0 only the colour changes and the foam keeps mirroring like water, which makes it look like a bright film again.
value.FOAM_MATT.0 = Off (mirrors like water)
value.FOAM_MATT.40 =§aSlight
value.FOAM_MATT.85 =默认
value.FOAM_MATT.100 = Fully dull

option.FOAM_WOBBLE = Water: foam width variation
option.FOAM_WOBBLE.comment = How much the width of the surf zone varies along the shore. At a fixed width the foam is an exactly uniform border around the block silhouette and therefore traces the block grid - it then looks as if it were only clinging to the sides of the shore blocks. A coarse noise field makes the zone bulge in and out instead. 0 restores the old, uniform behaviour.
value.FOAM_WOBBLE.0 = Off (uniform border)
value.FOAM_WOBBLE.45 =§aSlight
value.FOAM_WOBBLE.90 =默认
value.FOAM_WOBBLE.150 = Strongly ragged
option.FOAM_HOLES = Water: bubble holes
option.FOAM_HOLES.comment = How deeply the fine bubble field tears holes into the foam. Without it the surf zone becomes a solid sheet right at the shore, because almost all of the noise there lies above the threshold - the grain is then only visible in the outer strip. With the holes the foam stays a structure in the middle too.
value.FOAM_HOLES.0 =关
value.FOAM_HOLES.30 =少量
value.FOAM_HOLES.55 =默认
value.FOAM_HOLES.80 = Very holey
option.FOAM_RELIEF = Water: bubble relief
option.FOAM_RELIEF.comment = How three-dimensional the bubbles look. A shading term is derived from the slope of the bubble field: flanks tilted towards the sun get brighter, those facing away darker. This is the cheapest route to a sense of depth, because the normal lighting could not do it - the foam is deliberately matt and therefore carries no specular highlight in which a relief could show.
value.FOAM_RELIEF.0 =平面模式
value.FOAM_RELIEF.35 =§aSlight
value.FOAM_RELIEF.70 =默认
value.FOAM_RELIEF.120 =强

option.WATER_HASH = Waves: noise mixing
value.WATER_HASH.0 = Legacy (sin)
value.WATER_HASH.1 = Precise
option.WATER_HASH.comment = How the waves' random values are generated. "Legacy" uses sin() on very large numbers; far from the world origin only a few distinct values remain and visible edges and lattice patterns appear on the water. "Precise" avoids sin() and stays clean everywhere.
option.WATER_NOISE = Water: noise type
option.WATER_NOISE.comment = Which kind of noise shapes the waves and the foam. Value noise is interpolated with smoothstep, whose derivative is exactly zero at every cell boundary - but the wave normal IS that derivative, so every cell boundary carries a flat line. Measured, the slope there is 6.5 times lower than at the cell centre. It shows up as a drifting grid of long rectangles, partly axis aligned and partly rotated by almost 37 degrees (the rotation the fBm applies per octave), and it is worst in the evening when the reflection reacts sharply to the normal. Gradient noise places a direction vector at each lattice point instead of a random value; its derivative there is neither zero nor discontinuous, so the grid disappears by construction. It costs the same and matches the standard deviation of value noise to within a thousandth, so the tuning does not shift.
value.WATER_NOISE.0 = Value noise (old, has grid)
value.WATER_NOISE.1 = Gradient noise (default)

option.WATER_DEBUG = Water diagnostics
option.WATER_DEBUG.comment = Shows the individual quantities of the water surface separately, to pin down visual defects. All modes draw opaque so the ground below does not blend in. 1 water path - optical depth to the bottom, black near to white from eight blocks; shows steps in the ground. 2 surface opacity. 3 wave normal as colour; a grid from the noise interpolation would show here. 4 lightmap, sky light red and block light green; Minecraft supplies these per vertex, so steps at block edges show up here. 5 scattered water colour without reflection. 6 contribution of the reflection approximation. 7 foam mask alone. 8 Fresnel, the reflected fraction from the view angle.
value.WATER_DEBUG.0 =关
value.WATER_DEBUG.1 = 1 water path
value.WATER_DEBUG.2 = 2 opacity
value.WATER_DEBUG.3 = 3 wave normal
value.WATER_DEBUG.4 = 4 lightmap
value.WATER_DEBUG.5 = 5 water colour
value.WATER_DEBUG.6 = 6 reflection
value.WATER_DEBUG.7 = 7 foam
value.WATER_DEBUG.8 = 8 Fresnel
value.WATER_DEBUG.9 = 9 sun visibility at the bottom
value.WATER_DEBUG.10 = 10 caustics at the bottom
value.WATER_DEBUG.11 = 11 contribution of composite
value.WATER_DEBUG.12 = 12 refraction offset
value.WATER_DEBUG.13 = 13 normal as composite reads it
value.WATER_DEBUG.14 = 14 macro normal alone
value.WATER_DEBUG.15 = 15 micro waves alone
value.WATER_DEBUG.16 = 16 view direction
value.WATER_DEBUG.17 = 17 ground-plane coordinate (coarse)
value.WATER_DEBUG.18 = 18 wave height instead of slope
value.WATER_DEBUG.19 = 19 ground-plane coordinate (512x finer)
value.WATER_DEBUG.20 = 20 shore distance (red new, green legacy, blue sampling)
option.EMISSIVE_PEAK = Glowing blocks: peak brightness
value.EMISSIVE_PEAK.0 = Off (unlimited)
value.EMISSIVE_PEAK.140 = Dimmed
value.EMISSIVE_PEAK.170 = Normal (default)
value.EMISSIVE_PEAK.200 =明亮
value.EMISSIVE_PEAK.240 =极亮
option.EMISSIVE_PEAK.comment = Upper limit for lava, fire and lamps. Since 0.5.4 the image works in real HDR; unlimited, lava was so bright that the tonemapper bleached it to pure white. Only the brightness is compressed, the hue is kept. Higher = brighter; from about 200 the core turns increasingly white.
option.FOG_BORDER = Render-distance fog: start
value.FOG_BORDER.60 = 60 % (lots of fog)
value.FOG_BORDER.72 = 72 % (old)
value.FOG_BORDER.80 = 80 % (default)
value.FOG_BORDER.88 = 88 % (little fog)
option.FOG_BORDER.comment = At which fraction of the view distance the fog at the world edge begins; it always ends at 95 %. Higher = the fog starts later and is narrower, but hides the chunk edge less well. Applies to water too since 0.5.4.
option.CIRRUS_AMOUNT = Cirrus: amount
value.CIRRUS_AMOUNT.0 =关
value.CIRRUS_AMOUNT.25 =少量
value.CIRRUS_AMOUNT.50 =中（默认）
value.CIRRUS_AMOUNT.75 =大量
value.CIRRUS_AMOUNT.100 =极多
option.CIRRUS_AMOUNT.comment = High, fibrous ice clouds at about 1400 blocks (6-12 km in reality), as a flat layer behind the heap clouds. Cheap: no volume march. Temporary until the high layer of the sky rebuild is done. Hidden during rain.
